    "message": {
     "content": "### The Short Verdict\n**This is an exceptional, top-of-market offer (90th+ percentile for Google L6 PM).** \n\nYou should **not walk away**, and you are in a prime position to **accept**. Because you do not have competing offers and Google is already at the very top of their standard L6 bands, aggressive negotiation is unnecessary and carries little leverage. However, a polite, one-time closing ask is possible if you want to confirm you left nothing on the table.\n\n---\n\n### Offer Breakdown & Comparison\n\n#### 1. Total Compensation Comparison\n* **Meta (Current):** ~$526,000/yr (Base: $240k | Bonus: ~$36k | RSU: ~$250k).\n* **Google (Year 1):** **$847,000 \u2013 $954,000+** \n  * *Base:* $260,000\n  * *Target Bonus (20%):* $52,000\n  * *Sign-on:* $200,000\n  * *Equity (Year 1):* Google commonly uses a front-loaded vesting schedule (such as 33% / 33% / 22% / 12% or 38% / 32% / 20% / 10%). \n    * If 33% Year 1: **$442,200** stock vest $\\rightarrow$ **~$954,200 Year 1 TC**.\n    * Even if evenly distributed (25%/yr): **$335,000** stock vest $\\rightarrow$ **~$847,000 Year 1 TC**.\n* **Google (4-Year Annualized Average):** **~$697,000/yr** (excluding annual equity refreshes).\n\n#### 2. Component Analysis\n* **Base Salary ($260,000):** Sits at the **88th percentile** of the posted salary range ($183,000 \u2013 $271,000).\n* **Sign-on Bonus ($200,000):** Extremely generous for an L6 PM. Typical Google L6 sign-on bonuses without strong competing offers range between $50,000 and $100,000. This was likely structured to make you completely whole on unvested Meta equity and secure an immediate acceptance.\n* **RSUs ($1,340,000 / 4 yrs = $335,000/yr):** The median equity for a Google L6 PM in the Bay Area is typically between **$200,000 and $250,000/year**. An initial grant of $1.34M places you at the top tier of L6 equity bands.\n\n---\n\n### Should You Negotiate?\n\n#### The Reality on Leverage\nYou have no competing offers, and Google has already presented an offer that exceeds your Meta compensation by **$300,000+ in Year 1** and **~$170,000+/year on average** before refreshes. Google\u2019s compensation committee has already approved an offer near band maximums.\n\n#### Strategy Options\n\n#### Option A: Accept with Enthusiasm (Recommended)\nSince Google is your top choice, the team and role are exciting, and the package is already top-of-market, accepting directly builds strong goodwill with your hiring manager and team from day one.\n\n#### Option B: The \"One-Shot Closing\" Ask (Low Risk)\nIf you prefer to ask for a small bump to ensure no value was left behind, make a single, precise request tied to an immediate commitment to sign. \n\n* **What to target:**\n  * **Base Salary:** Ask for the top of the posted range (**$270,000** vs. $260,000).\n  * **Equity:** Ask for an even **$1,400,000** (+$60k total / +$15k/yr).\n* **Sample Framing:**\n  > *\"Thank you again for putting this package together. I am genuinely excited about the team and the scope of this role, and Google remains my top choice. If we can get the base salary to the top of the posted range at $270,000 (or round the equity grant to $1.4M), I am ready to sign the offer immediately.\"*\n\n---\n\n### Final Details to Clarify Before Signing\n1. **Vesting Schedule:** Confirm the exact vesting cadence (e.g., front-loaded 33/33/22/12 vs. 25% flat per year, and whether it vests monthly after month 1).\n2. **Sign-on Terms:** Confirm payment timing (first paycheck) and clawback terms (standard is a 12-month prorated repayment if you leave voluntarily).\n3. **Refresh Eligibility:** Confirm the review cycle timeline for your first annual refresher grant.",
